$CurrentArticles = @(
  @{
    Slug = 'custom-apparel-size-stickers-guide'
    Title = 'Custom Apparel Size Stickers for Faster Packing'
    Description = 'Plan apparel size stickers around folded garments, polybags, size variants, peel behavior and packing checks before ordering.'
    Category = 'Apparel packing'
    CardDescription = 'Make sizes easy to pick without leaving adhesive trouble on a garment or bag.'
    Alt = 'Original S M and L size stickers with colorful garment icons on folded tees and clear polybags'
    Caption = 'The size mark must be visible to the picker and sensible to the person opening the pack.'
    Published = '2026-10-01'
    Faq = @(
      @('Should a size sticker go on the garment or the polybag?', 'Start with the intended packing workflow. A polybag mark avoids direct fabric contact; a garment sticker needs a suitable sample test on the actual fabric and finish.'),
      @('Can every size use the same color?', 'It can, but a distinct size cue helps picking. Keep the printed size legible without relying on color alone.'),
      @('What information is needed for a quote?', 'Share pack photos, application surface, sticker dimensions, size list, quantities by size and any removal requirement.')
    )
    Body = @'
  <p>A size sticker is a small thing until a packing table has six piles of nearly identical shirts. The packer wants to spot M without unfolding every garment. The buyer does not want a sticky patch on the fabric when the customer opens the bag. I would settle placement before discussing a fashionable color palette.</p>
  <p>Decide whether the mark belongs on the garment, the polybag or a hang tag. Those are different surfaces and different removal expectations. If the sticker touches fabric, test it on the actual textile, print and finish. If it goes on a bag, test it after the garment is folded and the bag is filled; the useful visible area may be smaller than the flat bag suggests.</p>
  <h2>Choose the Surface Before the Artwork</h2>
  <table><thead><tr><th>Placement</th><th>Useful when</th><th>Check first</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Polybag front</td><td>Staff pick sealed sizes from shelves</td><td>Visibility after folding and boxing</td></tr><tr><td>Garment wrap</td><td>There is no outer bag</td><td>Release from the actual fabric</td></tr><tr><td>Hang tag</td><td>A tag already carries product data</td><td>Legibility and attachment position</td></tr></tbody></table>
  <p>Removable does not mean universally residue-free. Heat, storage time, fabric texture and printing on the garment can change the outcome. Ask for a sample of the proposed construction, apply it as the packing team would and remove it after a realistic storage interval. No generic adhesive claim replaces that check.</p>
  <h2>Build a Size System That Can Be Picked Quickly</h2>
  <p>An original shirt icon and a large letter can work better than decorative lettering. Put the size first, then any collection name or variant detail. Use contrast and a clear shape so a picker can tell XS from XL at arm's length. Color can reinforce the distinction, but the letter must stand on its own for monochrome printing or color-vision differences.</p>
  <p>If a range includes numeric, international and local sizes, the brand must approve the mapping. A print supplier should not infer that one market's M equals another's. Keep a version sheet with the exact text and quantity for every size. It is the quiet spreadsheet that prevents a beautiful stack of wrong labels.</p>
  <h2>Proof the Finished Pack, Not a Loose Sticker</h2>
  <p>Fold one garment per size, pack it normally and line the samples up as warehouse staff would see them. Check whether the size is visible under a barcode label, fold or seam. Then open a sample pack: does the sticker come off cleanly, and is the unboxing experience acceptable? If direct fabric contact is unnecessary, I would usually begin with the bag or tag.</p>
  <ul class="check-list"><li>Photograph the final folded garment and bag</li><li>Confirm sticker surface and removal expectation</li><li>List exact size text and count by variant</li><li>Proof the smallest size mark at actual dimensions</li><li>Run a sample peel and storage check</li><li>Verify visibility on a packed shelf and in a shipping carton</li></ul>
  <p>Use the <a href="/blog/removable-vs-permanent-stickers/">removable adhesive guide</a> for the material conversation and the <a href="/blog/custom-sticker-reorder-checklist/">reorder checklist</a> for version control. Send the pack photo and size matrix through the <a href="/label-specification-planner/">label specification planner</a>. The right size sticker saves a decision at the packing bench instead of creating another one.</p>
'@
  },
  @{
    Slug = 'custom-produce-clamshell-labels-guide'
    Title = 'Custom Produce Clamshell Labels: Vent, View and Lot'
    Description = 'Plan produce clamshell labels around clear lids, vents, condensation, product visibility and owner-approved lot information.'
    Category = 'Fresh produce packaging'
    CardDescription = 'Keep fresh produce visible while giving the packer a workable area for identity and lot details.'
    Alt = 'Original illustrated strawberry and blueberry labels applied to clear produce clamshells with visible vent slots'
    Caption = 'A clamshell label should leave the product, vents and opening action clear.'
    Published = '2026-10-01'
    Faq = @(
      @('Can a label cover the clamshell vents?', 'Do not assume so. Mark the vents and hinge on the real filled pack, then place the label outside functional areas.'),
      @('Is a waterproof label necessary for refrigerated produce?', 'The answer depends on the pack surface, condensation and handling. Test the proposed material on the actual chilled clamshell.'),
      @('Who approves origin and lot information?', 'The produce brand or packer approves traceability, origin and market-specific wording. Printing checks the supplied version and legibility.')
    )
    Body = @'
  <p>Fresh berries do some of their own selling. A shopper sees color and condition through the lid before reading a slogan. On a packing line, though, the pack still needs a clear product identity and the correct lot information. A label that covers the best view or a vent has solved the wrong problem.</p>
  <p>I would take the actual filled clamshell, close it and mark three things with removable tape: the clear viewing area, the vent openings and the hinge or latch. Only then is there a sensible label footprint. Empty packaging drawings rarely show how fruit rises into the lid or how a worker grips it.</p>
  <h2>Keep Functional Areas Clear</h2>
  <table><thead><tr><th>Pack feature</th><th>Buyer question</th><th>Proof check</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Clear lid</td><td>How much produce stays visible?</td><td>View a filled pack at shelf distance</td></tr><tr><td>Vent slots</td><td>Can the sticker block airflow?</td><td>Mark and avoid every vent</td></tr><tr><td>Hinge and latch</td><td>Can the pack open normally?</td><td>Open a labeled sample</td></tr><tr><td>Traceability area</td><td>Where is the lot printed or added?</td><td>Check the final packing step</td></tr></tbody></table>
  <p>A small illustrated fruit mark can make variants recognizable without hiding the produce. Original berry drawings, a strong variety name and a restrained color cue are enough. Save space for mandatory or owner-approved information; do not squeeze it into decorative type after the shape is finalized.</p>
  <h2>Test on a Chilled, Handled Pack</h2>
  <p>A clear plastic lid can have a different surface from a paper carton. Condensation, cold storage and wiping all matter. Ask for a trial label and apply it at the point in the workflow where staff will actually apply it. Then chill, handle and open the pack under realistic conditions. A dry sample on a desk is only an artwork proof.</p>
  <p>There is no universal promise that a material will stay attached on every clamshell. The pack resin, surface treatment and temperature need to be known. If the label will bridge a lid seam as a seal, define whether it is decorative, a closure aid or a tamper signal; those are different jobs.</p>
  <h2>Give Lot Data a Place Before Printing</h2>
  <p>Some packers add the lot later with a printer or secondary sticker. Others provide the data in the artwork. Agree on who owns the data, where it goes and whether every batch requires a variable field. Do not print an attractive but fictional origin or harvest date. The brand or packer must approve the claims and wording for the target market.</p>
  <ul class="check-list"><li>Send photos of the exact filled clamshell</li><li>Measure the usable lid area in millimeters</li><li>Mark vents, hinge, latch and viewing window</li><li>Identify who supplies lot and origin information</li><li>Test a sample after chilling and normal handling</li><li>Check the labeled pack in its transport carton</li></ul>
  <p>The <a href="/blog/custom-takeout-container-labels-guide/">food container label guide</a> explains another clear-pack placement problem; the <a href="/blog/sticker-adhesive-surface-matching-guide/">surface matching guide</a> helps frame adhesion questions. Upload the filled-pack photos and approved copy through the <a href="/label-specification-planner/">specification planner</a>. Let the fruit remain the first thing a buyer sees.</p>
'@
  },
  @{
    Slug = 'custom-refill-jar-labels-guide'
    Title = 'Custom Refill Jar Labels: Removal and Reuse'
    Description = 'Plan refill jar labels for bulk food shops around glass washing, clean removal, changing contents and readable customer information.'
    Category = 'Refill packaging'
    CardDescription = 'Decide what stays on the jar and what must change at every refill.'
    Alt = 'Original illustrated grain and coffee labels on reusable glass refill jars beside a washing station'
    Caption = 'A refill label has to work on the second use, not only the first shelf photograph.'
    Published = '2026-10-01'
    Faq = @(
      @('Will a removable label always wash off cleanly?', 'No. Glass preparation, adhesive, water temperature, dwell time and washing method affect removal. Test the exact construction in the intended process.'),
      @('Can one jar label serve several foods?', 'A fixed brand mark can remain, but product identity and owner-approved information must match the actual contents. Plan a controlled way to replace variable details.'),
      @('Should the label go on the lid or jar?', 'Choose based on the handling and washing sequence. The lid and jar may be separated, so do not put essential identity in a place that can be mismatched.')
    )
    Body = @'
  <p>A refill shop can make a glass jar look lovely once. The harder job is making it clear and usable after the jar is emptied, washed and filled again. If a paper label leaves a stubborn rectangle on the glass, the next refill starts with scraping. If the label is too easy to remove, it may fail while the customer is carrying the jar home.</p>
  <p>I would separate two questions: what information belongs to the reusable container, and what information belongs to this fill. A permanent decorative mark might identify the shop. The food name, batch or other approved details may need to change. Designing those as one inseparable label can create avoidable waste and confusion.</p>
  <h2>Map the Jar's Second Life</h2>
  <table><thead><tr><th>Moment</th><th>What to inspect</th><th>Possible choice</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>First fill</td><td>Curvature and clear viewing area</td><td>Front identity plus separate detail area</td></tr><tr><td>Handling</td><td>Grip, condensation and abrasion</td><td>Test material on a filled jar</td></tr><tr><td>Emptying</td><td>How the old contents are identified</td><td>Clear removal instruction</td></tr><tr><td>Washing</td><td>Water, detergent and temperature</td><td>Trial the exact removal process</td></tr><tr><td>Next fill</td><td>How new data replaces old data</td><td>Approved version or variable label</td></tr></tbody></table>
  <p>Use the actual jar shape and fill level. A label that wraps around a short, broad jar may hide most of the contents. A small original illustration of grain, beans or tea can identify the category while leaving a window onto the real product. Avoid assigning a fixed food illustration to a container intended for many different foods.</p>
  <h2>Choose Removal by Testing, Not by Name</h2>
  <p>Ask what the shop actually does: hand wash, dishwasher, soak, scrape or return to a central wash. Apply a proposed sample to the cleaned production glass, let it sit for the expected use period and then run that process. Check both the label's hold during use and the residue afterward. A material called removable may behave differently after heat or months on a shelf.</p>
  <p>If the shop expects customers to relabel at home, legibility and easy handling matter. A small writable area may be more useful than a dense printed panel. The food business remains responsible for required contents, allergen, origin and date information in each market; the print proof should use only supplied, approved copy.</p>
  <h2>Keep the Refill Routine Simple</h2>
  <ul class="check-list"><li>Photograph the actual jar and lid together</li><li>Identify information that changes with each fill</li><li>Measure the clear viewing area in mm</li><li>Test grip, storage and the real washing method</li><li>Check removal residue after a realistic dwell time</li><li>Approve copy and version control for every product</li></ul>
  <p>Read the <a href="/blog/removable-vs-permanent-stickers/">removable versus permanent guide</a> before choosing a construction, and compare the <a href="/blog/custom-spice-jar-labels-guide/">spice jar label guide</a> for smaller containers. Send jar photos and the wash routine through the <a href="/label-specification-planner/">specification planner</a>. A useful refill label respects the next person who has to remove it.</p>
'@
  },
  @{
    Slug = 'custom-electronics-serial-number-labels-guide'
    Title = 'Custom Electronics Serial Labels: Data and Placement'
    Description = 'Plan electronics serial number labels around variable data, barcode verification, enclosure finish and final inspection.'
    Category = 'Electronics identification'
    CardDescription = 'Connect the data file, print proof and physical device before a serial label reaches packing.'
    Alt = 'Original electronics device labels with sample serial text and 2D codes on unbranded enclosures at a workbench'
    Caption = 'The serial number is useful only when it matches the device and remains readable in its final position.'
    Published = '2026-10-01'
    Faq = @(
      @('Can serial numbers be changed after the labels are printed?', 'Not on a fixed print run. Approve the data source, numbering rules and replacement procedure before producing variable labels.'),
      @('Does a readable barcode on screen guarantee scanning?', 'No. Test printed codes at final size with the actual scanner and after application to the real enclosure.'),
      @('Can an ordinary sticker serve as a rating plate?', 'Do not assume so. Any required safety, durability or regulatory marking needs an application-specific review and owner-approved content.')
    )
    Body = @'
  <p>An electronics label is easy to underestimate because the artwork may be mostly numbers. At final assembly, however, one mismatched serial can make support, inventory and warranty records harder to use. The label must be readable, correctly paired with the device and placed where a technician can find it without dismantling the product.</p>
  <p>I would begin with the data flow, not a material swatch. Who creates serials? Who prevents duplicates? When is a label matched to the assembled unit? A crisp print cannot repair an uncontrolled spreadsheet. The brand or manufacturer must own the identifier scheme and any regulatory or product-rating content.</p>
  <h2>Separate Fixed Artwork From Variable Data</h2>
  <table><thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Owner decision</th><th>Proof check</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Product identity</td><td>Model name and version</td><td>Match the enclosure and box</td></tr><tr><td>Serial number</td><td>Source, sequence and uniqueness</td><td>First, middle and last records</td></tr><tr><td>Barcode or 2D code</td><td>Data encoded and scanner type</td><td>Scan printed final-size samples</td></tr><tr><td>Other markings</td><td>Approved market-specific wording</td><td>Owner sign-off, not supplier invention</td></tr></tbody></table>
  <p>For a code, agree on the encoding and quiet zone. Check whether a human-readable serial sits nearby so a technician can recover the identity if a scanner is unavailable. Sample several data records, including the longest likely string. Testing one short demonstration number is not enough.</p>
  <h2>Place the Label on the Actual Enclosure</h2>
  <p>The flat-looking back of a device may have vents, screws, a curve or a recess. A powder-coated metal enclosure, textured plastic and glossy carton offer different surfaces. Measure the usable space, note heat and cleaning exposure, then apply a material sample in the actual assembly sequence. Keep a serial away from a seam that will be opened routinely.</p>
  <p>Do not use an attractive mockup with fake compliance logos to suggest the device has approvals. If a rating or safety mark is required, the responsible product owner supplies exact approved artwork and test requirements. This article concerns identification planning, not certification.</p>
  <h2>Build a Final Inspection That Finds Mismatches</h2>
  <p>Print a final-size sample and scan it after application, not only on a loose liner. Check the result against the visible serial and the device record. Decide what happens to a damaged or misprinted label and how that serial is retired or reissued. Those simple decisions protect the integrity of the data more than an elaborate graphic border.</p>
  <ul class="check-list"><li>Supply an approved data file and numbering rules</li><li>Confirm exact enclosure surface and placement photo</li><li>Check longest strings at final label size</li><li>Scan samples after application</li><li>Define duplicate, damage and reprint handling</li><li>Match device, label and carton records at inspection</li></ul>
  <p>The <a href="/blog/qr-code-stickers-scan-testing-guide/">code scan testing guide</a> goes deeper on printed codes; the <a href="/blog/sticker-adhesive-surface-matching-guide/">surface matching guide</a> frames enclosure trials. Send the approved layout, data rules and surface photo through the <a href="/label-specification-planner/">specification planner</a>. The best serial label is boring in precisely the ways a service team needs.</p>
'@
  },
  @{
    Slug = 'custom-bicycle-frame-decals-guide'
    Title = 'Custom Bicycle Frame Decals: Curves and Clearcoat'
    Description = 'Plan original bicycle frame decals around tube curves, application, abrasion, clearcoat compatibility and artwork proofing.'
    Category = 'Sporting goods decals'
    CardDescription = 'Proof the graphic on a real frame tube before committing to a long run of decals.'
    Alt = 'Original mountain line and compass bicycle decals applied to an unbranded matte green frame at a workshop'
    Caption = 'A flat decal file cannot show how an illustration wraps around a tapered tube.'
    Published = '2026-10-01'
    Faq = @(
      @('Can a flat decal fit every bicycle frame?', 'No. Tube diameter, taper, welds and curvature vary. Measure and proof the intended frames rather than assuming one shape fits all.'),
      @('Should decals be applied before or after clearcoat?', 'That depends on the frame finishing system. The frame manufacturer must approve the process and test compatibility with the proposed print and adhesive.'),
      @('Are all vinyl decals suitable for outdoor riding?', 'No universal durability claim is justified. Describe washing, sunlight, abrasion and storage, then test the proposed construction for the intended use.')
    )
    Body = @'
  <p>A bicycle frame offers a long, tempting space for a graphic. It is not a flat signboard. The down tube tapers, the seat tube meets welds and a design that reads from one side can disappear around the curve from the other. I would tape a full-size paper proof to the real frame before discussing whether the artwork is bold enough.</p>
  <p>For an original frame decal, think about the person applying it as well as the rider seeing it. A very long single piece may look elegant in a file and be awkward to align around bottle bosses or cable ports. A shorter, well-placed graphic can be easier to install consistently.</p>
  <h2>Measure the Tube and the Viewing Angle</h2>
  <table><thead><tr><th>Frame area</th><th>Physical issue</th><th>Proof action</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Down tube</td><td>Taper and cable ports</td><td>Wrap a full-size paper template</td></tr><tr><td>Seat tube</td><td>Welds and bottle mounts</td><td>Mark clear application zones</td></tr><tr><td>Chain stay</td><td>Frequent scuffing</td><td>Review abrasion exposure</td></tr><tr><td>Head tube</td><td>Tight curve</td><td>Check small artwork legibility</td></tr></tbody></table>
  <p>Photograph the frame from the usual side and three-quarter angle. Mark the usable length and the widest area where the graphic will remain visible. An original mountain line, compass motif or abstract route shape can be distinctive without copying a bicycle manufacturer's badge. Avoid placing critical text where it wraps out of sight.</p>
  <h2>Agree on the Finishing Sequence</h2>
  <p>Is the decal applied to a finished frame, under a clearcoat or as a temporary event graphic? Those are different constructions. A frame finisher needs to test any overcoat with the exact print, ink and adhesive; a print supplier should not promise compatibility from a generic material description. If the graphic sits on top of paint, test placement after the coating has cured according to the frame maker's process.</p>
  <p>Think about washing, road grit, rack contact and sunlight. The rider may care more about a rub point than a dramatic studio photograph. Request a proposed construction and test it on a representative painted surface with the expected cleaning and handling routine.</p>
  <h2>Make Installation Part of the Proof</h2>
  <p>A transfer liner can help position detailed shapes, but the application team still needs clear alignment references. Test the smallest piece and the longest piece on the intended tube. Look for edge lift, trapped air and distortion around taper. Decide whether the graphic will be installed at the factory, shop or by the rider; installation instructions should match that setting.</p>
  <ul class="check-list"><li>Send frame model photos without borrowed brand artwork</li><li>Measure target tubes and identify ports and bosses</li><li>Proof original art at full physical size</li><li>Confirm finish and installation sequence</li><li>Trial a sample on representative paint</li><li>Inspect from normal viewing angles after application</li></ul>
  <p>See the <a href="/blog/outdoor-sticker-durability-guide/">outdoor sticker durability guide</a> for exposure questions and the <a href="/blog/how-to-apply-stickers-without-bubbles/">application guide</a> for placement technique. Send tube measurements and original artwork through the <a href="/label-specification-planner/">specification planner</a>. The frame, not the monitor, gets the final vote.</p>
'@
  }
)
